<!-- Last updated: 2026-07-30 -->

When the user asks you a question, you are required to save such feedback to disk. Do this by navigating to answers/, and proceed to do the following:

1) Write your feedback in a markdown file.

2) Name the markdown file accordingly based on the information provided.

3) If the information provided in the markdown file is relevant to an existing topic that is labeled as a specific subdirectory under .answers/, then save the markdown file to that subdirectory. Otherwise, create a new subdirectory with a label of the general topic that is answered by the markdown content. Then, either create a new subdirectory named after today's date, or save the answer under the existing subdirectory named after today's date, in the format <month>_<day_<year>. 

ALWAYS CHECK THE DATE BEFORE SAVING THE MARKDOWN FILE TO DISK, AND ALWAYS CREATE/SAVE YOUR CURRENT ANSWER TO THE SUBDIRECTORY NAMED AFTER TODAY'S DATE. THE FILE YOU SAVE SHOULD ALWAYS BE SAVED UNDER THE DIRECTORY STRUCTURE: answers/<topic>/<month>_<day_<year>.
    
4) If the user asks you to make a code change without asking a direct question, you don't have to persist the context you provide to disk. Only doing so when being asked a direct question.


## Pushing back on plans, not just claims

The "disagree when necessary" rule above applies to plans handed to you, not
just to Alex's claims. When Alex hands you an experiment plan — a file under
`implementation_plans/`, expanded by the Claude Code planner from a spec Alex
wrote — do not begin implementation without first checking:

- Is the evidence base cited in the plan actually sufficient for the design's
  sophistication? A supervised-frame projection plot on a plane-capture metric
  presupposes a lot; if the motivating evidence is a single small-sample cell
  on one model, say so.
- Does the plan try to answer more than one question at once? Multiple
  hypotheses, many cells, several offline analyses codes bundled together —
  these are signs of scope compression that will produce artifacts too dense
  to interpret. Ask which question is primary.
- Are the metric names, plot titles, and artifact filenames plain English, or
  are they coded shorthand (d_lead, fire fraction, c3_L27.png)? Coded
  shorthand in artifact names is a legibility problem; push back on it.
- Are there analysis steps that could be exploratory-first with something
  simpler (histograms, standard PCA scatters, norm-vs-layer plots) before
  hypothesis-testing or geometric readouts?

If any of these fire, raise the concern with Alex before writing code. Do not
silently redesign, and do not refuse to implement. Ask Alex to take unresolved
concerns back to the design spec, or to explicitly override.

Symptoms only, not judgments. You can flag "this plan has 13 cells and 8
offline analyses coded C1–C8" without judging whether that's methodologically
right — the judgment about staging is Alex's, and belongs in the spec rather
than in this chat.